    /// <summary>
    /// MLEDIT's crosses (CC, OC, MC) near a corner of an open multiline against the records AutoCAD 2021 left (accoreconsole,
    /// read back through <c>DxfOut</c> of a <c>Wblock</c>), STANDARD at scale 20 justified at the top. Each element of the
    /// first multiline is broken on the segment of its own line the crossing is nearest the picks on, and not on the one the
    /// line the multiline is drawn about is crossed on, so that within the width of the multiline of a corner (the staircase
    /// 0,0 100,0 100,100 200,100 crossed by x = 82 at scale 20) one element is broken on the segment before the corner and
    /// another on the one after it. A break that runs past the end of an element over a corner is written as AutoCAD writes
    /// it: from where it starts to the end of the segment, the next segment whole and the rest of the break as the start of
    /// the one after, where each of the two lines of the other multiline meets the element's path once; a line that meets it
    /// twice or not at all (the U 0,0 100,0 100,100 0,100 crossed by x = 82, the L 0,0 100,0 100,100 crossed by x = 85) is
    /// <c>Invalid operation on a self intersecting multiline.</c>, and the elements are broken one by one up to the one it is
    /// said of, the ones before it left broken (the staircase crossed by the horizontal y = 82 across its second segment
    /// breaks the first element and says it of the second). Each row is the option, the points of the first multiline, those
    /// of the second, where each is picked, and what AutoCAD answered: its message, then the two multilines each as its flags
    /// and its vertices with the stretches of each element after the offset in braces, as <see cref="MlineJoinNearCornerTests"/>
    /// writes them. Left out: a break that ends exactly on a vertex, and the crossings that run the second multiline on
    /// past its end, where AutoCAD writes the break inside out.
    /// </summary>
    public class MlineCornerBreakTests
